module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.policy_mechanism_adjudicates_the_merge_that_supersedes_it

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data policy_mechanism_adjudicates_the_merge_that_supersedes_it: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "policy_mechanism_adjudicates_the_merge_that_supersedes_it" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a policy mechanism that ships in the tree it adjudicates, so the one change that supersedes it is adjudicated by the superseded version** (a merge driver, a hook, a lint config, a formatter, a codegen template, a CI entry point -- anything the toolchain reads from the WORKING TREE at the moment it acts. The version that acts is the version already installed, so on every change that leaves the mechanism alone its instruction is current, and on the single change that REPLACES the mechanism its instruction is guaranteed to be the retired one. ",

    "**THIS IS AN ANTI-CORRELATION OF ONE, NOT A TIMING HAZARD, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DISCRIMINATOR.** The mechanism is wrong exactly when it matters and only when it matters. There is no bad draw to be unlucky with and no window to shorten: the more significant the policy change, the more certainly the author is handed the old policy. A rate cannot be improved because the rate is one. ",

    "IT IS DISTINCT FROM `verification_bound_to_the_revision_it_started_on` AND MERGING THEM LOSES BOTH. There the SUBJECT moves under a STABLE OBSERVER -- a run spans a tree change and the result binds to the wrong revision -- so it bites by timing and most runs escape it. HERE THE SUBJECT IS FINE AND THE OBSERVER IS STALE, BY CONSTRUCTION. Both halves are inverted, which is why a variant section on a row whose entire content is `results are bound to a revision` would leave this class findable only by someone who already knew it. ",

    "IT IS NOT `stable_citation_mutable_referent`, THE NEAREST STRUCTURAL NEIGHBOUR. That row is about an IDENTIFIER addressing a CONTAINER that serves whatever currently occupies it; here nothing is cited at all, and the stale thing is an EXECUTABLE shipped in the tree it judges. It is not `authority_merges_unprotected_while_its_projection_is_guarded`, which is about which SIDE of a derivation a policy protects, nor `admission_predicate_evidenced_from_inside_its_own_subject`, which is about a surface that cannot represent its predicate being false -- here the surface is perfectly capable of saying the right thing and is simply one version behind. ",

    "**WHAT MAKES IT LOAD-BEARING IS THAT NOTHING IN THE OUTPUT DISTINGUISHES THE RETIRED RECIPE FROM THE LIVE ONE.** Real mechanism, real refusal, real located instructions, every intermediate signal correct, one version stale. An author who follows it does substantial work down a route the merge is retiring and ends with a coherent, well-evidenced account of why -- which is the same reason it survives review, since the reviewer reads the same output. ",

    "SPECIMEN, gunbc#10311 against gunbc#10302 at `d6fb9d61c8`. Integrating main produced a `GeneratedArtifactConcurrentDivergence` refusal on `docs/design-failure-modes.md` whose printed repair route was a four-step LOCAL whole-tree regeneration -- roughly twenty-five minutes. The merge being performed was precisely the one that DELEGATES regeneration of the two design-ledger projections to `heal-generated-artifacts`, and git had executed the branch's own pre-merge copy of `.githooks/generated-artifact-merge`. The live route is three steps and regenerates nothing locally. ",

    "**MITIGATION, STATED AS AN EXECUTABLE PROCEDURE RATHER THAN AS VIGILANCE: when a merge changes a policy mechanism the toolchain itself executes, ABORT, INSTALL THE INCOMING VERSION FIRST AS ITS OWN COMMIT, THEN RE-RUN THE MERGE AND READ THE NEW DIAGNOSTIC.** In the specimen that is `bc402f4d`, and the re-run printed the delegated route. A row that says only `beware` leaves the next lane to rediscover the fix at a conflict, under time pressure, holding instructions that look authoritative. ",

    "**THREE ARMS, REACHED INDEPENDENTLY BY THREE LANES INSIDE ONE HOUR, NONE ABLE TO FIND THE CLASS IN THE ROSTER -- WHICH IS ITSELF THE EVIDENCE THAT IT IS A DISTINCT IDENTITY.** They differ in WHO is misled and in which direction, and a row carrying only the first teaches a reader to distrust the instruction when the actual defect is the VERSION. ",

    "ARM ONE, THE AUTHOR FOLLOWING THE RETIRED ROUTE (gunbc#10311, the specimen above): cost is one wasted regeneration. Expensive and self-limiting. ",

    "ARM TWO, THE MIRROR, AND IT IS WORSE (snappy-koi-879, gunbc#10271, hit within minutes and without knowledge of the first): the author was about to report a LANDED CAPABILITY AS NOT HAVING TAKEN EFFECT -- telling a manager that the emitted diagnostic contradicted the claim that delegation had merged, from a completely correct reading of a stale instrument. The forward arm costs a regeneration; THE MIRROR ARM RETRACTS A SHIPPED CAPABILITY ON EVIDENCE THAT LOOKS IMPECCABLE, and a false negative about a capability propagates: it gets relayed, it stops other lanes adopting the new route, and correcting it requires someone to disbelieve a direct instrument reading. In their words, the delivery and the invocation are THE SAME OPERATION, so it is not a race and cannot be retried into correctness. ",

    "ARM THREE, THE ENFORCEMENT INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN THE AUTHOR (neat-swift-219, a merge-readiness gate probing candidate heads with `git merge-tree --write-tree` from a worktree several commits behind main): the gate deciding whether anything merges was adjudicating heads against a policy the repository had already retired. **ITS VERDICT FIELD WAS IDENTICAL UNDER BOTH VERSIONS -- REFUSED -- AND ONLY THE INSTRUCTIONS DIFFERED, SO THE ARM THAT DECIDES WAS UNCHANGED WHILE THE ARM THAT EXPLAINS WAS WRONG.** Nothing in the output could have revealed it and a verdict-only reader would never have looked. This arm generalises furthest: any long-lived checker holding a cached copy of a versioned policy inherits it, and the more automated the checker the less likely anyone re-reads its prose. ",

    "**THE REMEDY IS snappy-koi-879's AND IT HAS A RECEIPT FROM SOMEONE WHO IS NOT ITS AUTHOR:** extract the mechanism from the incoming side and point at it with a one-shot config override -- `git -c merge.generated-artifact.driver=\"bash <extracted> %O %A %B %P\" merge ...` -- touching no tracked file. neat-swift-219 executed it independently and their gate immediately printed the delegated route in place of the retired one; it now re-extracts on every probe rather than caching. THE GENERAL FORM, IN THEIR WORDS: WHENEVER THE MERGE CARRIES THE TOOL, THE TOOL MUST BE TAKEN FROM THE INCOMING SIDE BEFORE IT JUDGES, NEVER FROM THE WORKTREE. ",

    "**WHY THE OVERRIDE BEATS THE OBVIOUS FIX, AND THIS IS THE SENTENCE THAT SAVES THE NEXT LANE AN HOUR.** The instinctive repair is to sync the worktree to main so the tool is current -- and doing so CONFLICTED on the very roster carrier the tool adjudicates, which two other lanes were appending to at that moment. So the obvious fix for a stale policy tool drags the fixer into the exact contention the tool exists to arbitrate. The override is not merely more elegant: it is the only version that does not put the checker into the queue it is checking. The install-first commit in the specimen works for an author who is merging anyway; the override is what a CHECKER must use, because a checker has no business acquiring a merge. ",

    "CROSS-REFERENCE WITHOUT MERGER: this shares a SIGNATURE with the upload-side defect where the worse the workflow breakage the less repair material escaped. Both degrade in the wrong direction -- reliability falling as stakes rise -- and the mechanisms are unrelated. The shared signature is the thing to pattern-match on; a common row over it would name a shape, not a class. ",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: MITIGATABLE, and only by an author who happens to notice that the diff touches the mechanism. Nothing refuses, nothing warns, and the instruction is delivered with full authority. ",

    "ATTAINABLE CEILING: STRUCTURALLY GUARANTEED. Membership is decidable at the moment of the merge -- the merge itself knows whether the incoming side changes the mechanism's own path -- so a mechanism can refuse rather than instruct when it is about to be replaced. It does not reach impossibility while the mechanism is a tree-resident file that the toolchain resolves by path, because the invalid state remains perfectly writable; making it impossible would require the toolchain to resolve the mechanism from the MERGED authority rather than from the worktree, which is not ours to change. ",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMED AS THE CAPABILITY: A TREE-RESIDENT POLICY MECHANISM THAT DETECTS ITS OWN PATH AMONG THE INCOMING CHANGES AND REFUSES WITH A LOCATED DIAGNOSTIC NAMING THE SUPERSEDING VERSION, INSTEAD OF EMITTING ITS OWN INSTRUCTIONS -- sufficient for an author integrating a policy change to be STOPPED rather than misdirected, and sufficient for the retired recipe never to be printed as if it were live. `Document the install-first procedure` is the artifact-grained version and discharges nothing: it is satisfied by prose that the misdirected author has no reason to consult, because from inside the failure nothing looks wrong.)",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
